  abstract = {My job is to open up and introduce this exciting conference. My wife is a microbiologist and a chef and now has a cooking school in Charlotte called, “The Perfect Dish.” We did not realize in the Internet age that was not necessarily a great name to set up as a Web site, so we get some interesting e-mails.In preparing for this presentation it seemed like we could have some fun making useful comparisons between becoming a chef and becoming a pathologist. Early in our medical training we had food-related terms that were ingrained in us comparing medical problems such as the nutmeg liver for cirrhosis, and so forth.This presentation will have some fun demonstrating the transformation of a great cook, my wife, into a chef and using analogies to the transformation going on in medicine and specifically in pathology. My wife was always a phenomenal cook. Her passion for cooking started early, for example on our second date, I was 17, she was 16, we went out to a movie and I said, “Let's go get a pizza,” and she said, “No, let's go to my house and I'll make you a pizza.” She realized and I quickly recognized after she went to culinary school that there is, in fact, a huge difference between being a cook and being a chef. So what does this have to do with our conference? We will return to this question after a short discussion of health care today.What is changing in our health care environment? We have patients. Patients are changing. They have access to much more knowledge. I am not going to say they are more knowledgeable but they certainly have access to much more information that they will bring to you and other physicians they encounter. The education of our citizens in science is pretty low. They thus have access to a lot of scientific information that they need help to understand.Let us start with the challenges facing the primary care physician or should we say a primary care provider? They are changing. Who are these people who the patient first encounters? How is care delivered? Where and by whom? We heard a little bit about that in Jason Hwang's presentation. We are having mind-boggling changes in technology and in every specialty in medicine yet the primary care provider's responsibility to order the right diagnostic studies and then to understand them remains critical to patient care, and how are they being educated as it relates to all these new innovations?What do consumers want from our health care system? Of course, they want high quality. Everybody wants high quality. In health care, however, they have a little bit of a problem defining high quality as it relates to “good health care.” Usually consumers define good health care by the politeness of the health care provider or whether they were seen quickly. They want convenience. They want fast and accurate information and they want trust and confidence. They want to trust and have confidence in those caring for them.That is a problem today in medicine because as patients get shifted around from physician to physician it becomes very difficult to have trust in your health care provider.What do physicians want? Those of you who have hired physicians in the last several years realize every new generation of physicians are told by the previous generation that they do not know as much. There is too much knowledge; there is too much information. There is simply not enough time to learn it all. When you talk to the residents who are finishing their training today, regardless of whether its surgery, pathology, or whatever, their ability to get experience during their training is getting more and more difficult. Physicians, more than ever, want help and this is where pathologists can play an incredible role. They want fast and accurate results just like the patient. They want to make a diagnosis and help that patient. But that information has to be understandable and has to be helpful. Then the physician needs direction on appropriate therapy. The ability to keep up with all the changes in drug therapies is essentially impossible even in narrow specialties such as infectious disease. The physician today and even more so in the future will need a lot of help in determining appropriate medications for any given disease on any given patient. Here is where molecular diagnostics and targeted therapies will come into the picture and this should be in the realm of pathology. We will have to identify the patients who may benefit from the drug, the patients who may be harmed by the drug, and the patients in whom we are uncertain how the drug will work.In an April issue of The Wall Street Journal1 it was stated that most of the primary care being delivered in many communities is being delivered by nurse practitioners and physician assistants. In recent years, several doctoral programs for nursing have opened up. Students can graduate high school, attend a 5-year nursing program, and graduate with a PhD in nursing. This program produces nurses whose job will be to provide primary care, go to hospitals, see patients, and care for patients in the hospital. As Jason pointed out, we are not there yet, but when more and more medicine becomes rules based and the software and the information systems are available to help, many of the diseases that physicians deal with will be taken care of by these individuals.What do patients and physicians want? They want everything. It is not going to be good enough to provide them with simply an appetizer-size explanation. We and our patients know with the knowledge gained from the human genome project that each of us are individuals, very unique, and the information on each of us is going to increase significantly. Someone is going to have to be out there to help interpret these data and make meaning of the information, for if not put in context, the testing data can be very confusing and misleading.The whole spectrum of care, including screening, differential diagnosis, prognosis, treatment, and monitoring, does fall within the purview of our specialty.This raises big questions in our specialty now: Are we becoming too specialized? Are we becoming too narrow? Are we shrinking our scope of practice rather than expanding what we call pathology? Pathologists all over the country in the major medical centers, in small pathology practice, are thinking, Do I have to be a gastrointestinal pathologist? Do I need to have a dermatopathologist or will we lose all our skin biopsies?To illustrate the point, let me share with you an interesting experience I had after giving a talk in New York several weeks ago. One of the faculty asked if she could talk to me and she looked a little concerned. She said, “Dr Schwartz, after your talk you really scared the hell out of me.” In the lecture I was stressing that when we act as consultants we need the whole plate of knowledge about the specialized area, not just the appetizer portion of information; we need to expand our scope of practice, expand what we are doing. She said, “All I do is liver biopsies. I come in everyday and our hospital does about 250 liver transplants a year. I come in every day and I look at the liver biopsies and I make decisions regarding rejection and treatment and all that.” And I said, “No, that's not all you do, that's only one thing you do. You have one tool, a microscope, and you're looking at a biopsy, but what's in your head? I mean you know more about liver disease than anyone I would imagine in this institution.” She then asked another question, “Do you think there might be a molecular test, a serum test, that could be developed that would make what I do obsolete?” I said, “Yes, of course there will be a molecular test, so what. They're probably working on it right now. Why should that worry you? When that molecular laboratory produces that result, you will have access to that result. Why can't you walk across the hall to the clinical lab and find out what the serum studies are on this liver patient? Or look up the clinical lab studies on your computer? You've defined yourself too narrowly. You are a liver pathologist, but you're not a liver microscope slide pathologist.” That is one of the many things that we have to be concerned about.When you are trained as a gastrointestinal fellow, are you learning about everything associated with gastrointestinal disease? The clinical lab aspects, the molecular aspects, the image studies, the treatment aspects, or are you just taking a biopsy and sticking it under a microscope and saying, “Oh, yes, this is collagenous colitis or this is Crohn disease.” We are training our people too narrowly and missing out on phenomenal opportunities to be truly specialists in disease. Remember pathology is the study of disease.What barriers exist? Payment issues, apathy, information technology, gene patents, lack of agreement, education, lack of interoperability, and software issues. But most importantly mindsets. All these things can be overcome.Many of you have heard of the book, The Last Lecture,2 and the video as seen on YouTube. Randy Pausch, the professor who recently died, was from Carnegie Mellon and a computer software developer who was diagnosed with pancreatic cancer. In his lecture and subsequent book he speaks about the “brick walls” that we all confront. Payment information, technology, lack of agreement, education software issues, interoperability are all brick walls. The purpose of the brick wall is to find out who has the passion in what they do to get over or around or through the barriers. The people that get up and over it or around it move on to the next opportunity where they will encounter another brick wall and the process starts all over again.Are we responsible for just the appetizer looking at that liver biopsy or are we responsible for the whole enchilada? We are responsible for the whole enchilada—all the data on this patient that can help us put together a comprehensive report that becomes useful information for directing patient care.We have incredible opportunities to play in so many different roles if our mindset allows us to understand that we can play many different roles as pathologists. We can be innovators again, going back to Jason's remarks looking for new ways of doing things.Test providers, the interpreters, there is no limit but the chains we put on ourselves. Why aren't we interpreting more? Why do we just pump out data without interpreting it in correlation with everything else that is going on with the patient? We have access to that data. Increasingly we are having more and more access to all of the data. I would bet in many of your health systems you have access to all the images from radiology. What are you doing with that information? Are you correlating it with your information?Actually we do have some innovators who are beginning to integrate our lab data with information from other clinical areas, such as imaging and the pharmacy. You have a patient with a lymphoma, leukemia. He or she gets flow cytometry, a bone marrow, and chemistries, and the physician gets 45 pages of data, none of it integrated. There are some integrators out there who are beginning to integrate this. They focus on leukemias, lymphomas, and they are integrating all these data and those, in fact, are the mini-mills of pathology that Jason gave us as examples in his lecture. I love that analogy. I grew up in Youngstown, Ohio, which was a steel town, a big steel town. When I left to go off to college there were 160 000 people. There are now 69 000 people in Youngstown and little steel. I moved to Charlotte, North Carolina, where NUCOR Steel was just beginning and they were a mini-mill. I remember thinking when I got there, “Where is this steel mill? I do not see it.” Then I drove by this building and just like Jason said, it was like a warehouse. What kind of steel can they be making in there? This was many, many years ago. Of course, now they're a major worldwide steel company.The pathologist is a primary clinical consultant. Wow, Jason, you do not know how important it was when you welcomed us as clinicians. This is one of my big pet peeves. Pathologists are just as much clinicians defined as physicians who take care of patients as any other physician, but in our mindset we've separated ourselves out even by the use of our words when we say, clinicians, nonclinicians. That is all mindset. We have to become in our heads clinical consultants.As Mary Kass said in her remarks, we have all the ingredients to provide a significant impact on medical practice, but we, like the radiologists, have a big danger and that is the danger of defining ourselves by our tools. Are kitchen tools what make a chef a chef? I can tell you, I have been in a lot of really incredibly fancy kitchens that have all these stainless steel tools, but the individuals working there are not chefs. It is not about the tools. It is what is in our heads and the passion we bring to what we do everyday that defines us.I love the psychology of humans and how we can isolate ourselves from the reality around us. It is fascinating to me. I will go into one of my partner's offices and he or she will be looking at the microscope and using earplugs in a music player.My partner is getting a prostate specimen from a da Vinci robotic equipment, and on the belt is a Smartphone. He or she has a laptop and is looking in the computer for radiology images. I ask, “What do you think about digital pathology?” “Oh, it will never happen. Hundred years, never happen. Never happen.” I will look around and I will say what is going on in these people's heads? Look what they have already accepted. Why is it that we are willing to accept all this other stuff and yet the thing that we're using, the classic microscope, is a no way! Oh, no, no, this will never, ever change. It is insane! We must figure out a way to get through to our colleagues that the microscope is just another tool and it is going to be replaced by a far better tool.The perfect dish for pathologists requires the right mindset because I really believe that what makes us great pathologists is what is in our heads. What makes a chef a great chef is what is in his or her head. Have an open mind to new ideas, technologies, and ways of practice. Acknowledge market forces. Engage in the practice. Be life-long learners. I know it sounds silly, but a lot of studies have shown that within 5 years of finishing residency, most physicians are only subscribing to one, at the most, two journals. I often ask residents and faculty about whether they read The Wall Street Journal, Harvard Business Review, or Business Week. That is where all the innovations are being written about, that is where they are being talked about. “Oh, I don't have time for that.” Well, then you're going to be one of these people that Jason was talking about where all of a sudden there's not going to be a place for you.Develop new skills and leverage your old ones. We can all do that. Most of us have learned how to use a digital music player, or if we cannot use it we get one of our kids to use it, to show us how to do it. We have learned how to do this. Do you understand what the Internet was in 1990 and now what it is and yet we just act as though it was always present.Interact with patients and other physicians. This again goes back to what Jason said. You have to be part of a team. You have to have all the information you can and you will expand your value by influencing prognosis. We are going to have those tools. We are going to have those molecular tools and we are going to be hearing about that and the imaging tools to be able to do so much more than we are doing today in the next 2 days of lectures at Futurescape.Another issue: Students need to learn how to think critically, how to argue opposing ideas. It is important for them to learn how to think. You can always cook. I can tell you, I have been around to 20 plus medical training centers in the last 2 years and I hear the same thing over and over and over again; it is that basically our residents are being trained to cook, and only at a few places are they beginning to try to figure out how to make themselves involved and understand critical thinking because much of what they will learn will be obsolete in the next 5 to 10 years. This is as true in pathology as it is in the restaurant business.Futurescape 2008 offers all the ingredients to spice up your practice with new ideas and opportunities and that is the purpose of this Futurescape series. We have pathology's top chefs. The upcoming speakers you will hear will demonstrate new technologies and applications that will markedly expand the opportunities for us. Some of what you see will not look like pathology. It will not look like pathology. You will say, “Wait a minute, that looks like an nuclear magnetic resonance or that looks like chemistry. That's a graph. How can that be pathology? There's a chart there.” It is pathology and it is anatomic pathology, by the way. Some of our speakers are going to tell you how they have already placed these recipes into practice. They have already made this work. They have cooked it, they have baked it, it is working, it has been integrated into their system.You all know this guy, Anthony Bourdain. Both of my daughters are in New York. My wife and I go to his restaurant a lot. Here is his quote, “I keep coming back to the question, when is food good?” This is exactly what Mary Kass was commenting on before. A great chef is proud of what he or she does. Great physicians, great pathologists are proud of what they do. We are excited about what we do. We are excited with our creations. We are excited with the opportunities to do things in different ways and make new things. Actually, make new things out of the old things. Do things differently.One of the things I would say when my wife came back from her culinary school is, “This salad, it looks the same, but so much Or I would say a few this the same we had weeks or a few and the would “Yes, the same but it was It was a little bit That's how we have to be able to last is for the residents because I think this is really I love this always a cook, she would never call a of course, we all know she was a great chef. the residents who are in the or for the residents when you go remember this quote, “I was when I started then I just I think this is so true of us because most of us our training when we are about or on how it you, and how many you you It is important to get across to the people when you go back that they are just They have been up up and now they are going to have to learn how to become a a},
